WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.280
The results of the national elections in
Iraq were shocking, but they weren't

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.660
necessarily surprising. Less than 45
percent of eligible voters cast a ballot.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.260
Most didn't vote at all because they
were frustrated with the current crop of

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.350
leaders who consistently failed to
improve their lives.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.250
This explains why Muqtada Al-Sadr's
Sairoon Coalition appears to have won a

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.300
narrow plurality. Sadr has cultivated
his status as an arch Iraqi

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.070
nationalist and the champion of average
Iraqis. Even still, Sadr didn't win by

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.210
much, so his ability to determine the
governing coalition – let alone dictate

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.110
and dominate it – is going to be very
limited. Prime Minister Abadi could

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:44.579
easily retain power as a compromise
candidate, or the Iranians might squeeze

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:48.059
the various Shia parties until they
agree to Tehran's preferred candidate, as

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:53.100
they eventually did back in 2010. But for
my money, the most important question is

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.309
not who becomes prime minister right now.
It's what happens when the government

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:00.210
fails to deliver better governance,
because it's all too likely that they

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.229
will fail, especially since the United
States seems uninterested in providing

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:07.400
the help and guidance that will be
needed for them to succeed.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.980
Do you think that the Iraqi elections matter at all
for American interests? Let us know in

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.770
your comments. Also let us know what
other topics you'd like our scholars to

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.670
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like
and subscribe for more research and

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.689
videos from AEI.

